WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.180
Two growth factors switch on the exact same kinase, ERK, inside one cell.

2
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.060
Why does one order division while the other orders nerve-like branches?

3
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.860
If the molecule is identical, something else in the signal must carry the

4
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:19.860
difference.

5
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:25.703
Cells reuse a small toolkit of messengers, so identity is only part of the message.

6
00:00:27.003 --> 00:00:31.403
A cell also reads four features: how much, how long, how often, and where.

7
00:00:32.723 --> 00:00:37.503
Reading time takes a decoder, a downstream protein with its own built-in speed.

8
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:39.897
Start with how long.

9
00:00:41.337 --> 00:00:46.497
EGF turns ERK on briefly, while NGF keeps ERK on much longer.

10
00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:49.157
A cell has no stopwatch.

11
00:00:50.557 --> 00:00:56.237
Instead, it compares ERK against a delayed protein that the same signal switches on.

12
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.220
In fibroblasts, ERK switches on a gene called c-fos.

13
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.960
Building c-Fos protein takes time, and fresh c-Fos breaks down quickly.

14
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.700
Rapid destruction sounds wasteful, yet it works as a timer.

15
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.140
Only if ERK stays on until c-Fos appears can phosphates protect it.

16
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:20.763
Pause and test this timer.

17
00:01:22.043 --> 00:01:27.123
Suppose a mutation makes fresh c-Fos stable right away, with no phosphates needed.

18
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:32.583
After a brief EGF pulse, will c-Fos now build up, or still vanish?

19
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:44.607
It builds up, because without fast breakdown, c-Fos survives even after ERK shuts

20
00:01:44.607 --> 00:01:50.667
off. Brief and long signals now look alike, so the decoder has lost its clock.

21
00:01:52.007 --> 00:01:53.947
Now carry that idea to repeating spikes.

22
00:01:55.167 --> 00:02:00.567
Calcium often rises in brief spikes of similar height, and in many cells stronger

23
00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.487
cues send faster spikes.

24
00:02:03.727 --> 00:02:08.207
A cell counts them using a decoder that turns on fast and decays slowly.

25
00:02:09.727 --> 00:02:14.047
Picture a leaky bucket: rare cups drain away, while rapid cups stack higher.

26
00:02:14.047 --> 00:02:17.927
Each spike adds onto whatever activity remains.

27
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.920
The fraction left equals one -half to the power of gap divided by half time.

28
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.760
Take a 30-second half-time.

29
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.620
With spikes 120 s apart, 120 ÷ 30 is four half times.

30
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.300
One half four times leaves 1/16, about 6%.

31
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:44.420
percent. At 15 s apart, 15 ÷ 30 is half a half time.

32
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.740
One half to the power of one half leaves about 71%.

33
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:49.740
percent.

34
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.030
Duration and frequency follow one rule.

35
00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:59.370
Compare a signal against a decoder's delay or decay, and one messenger carries many

36
00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:05.490
orders. Next, read how real proteins do this, and meet one hormone that needs the

37
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:05.750
gaps.
